WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.420
Security-sensitive tokens require cryptographically secure randomness.

2
00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:17.800
In Node.js, that means using appropriate crypto APIs for random bytes, random integers, UUIDs, or token material instead of familiar JavaScript shortcuts.

3
00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:31.340
Math.random is not designed for session IDs, API keys, password reset links, invite links, CSRF tokens, OAuth state, authorization codes, or any value that protects access or workflow approval.

4
00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:35.460
Token length should match the threat model and lifetime.

5
00:00:35.560 --> 00:00:43.080
A password reset token sent in a link needs enough entropy to resist guessing until it expires and is used.

6
00:00:43.180 --> 00:00:49.900
An API key that may live for months needs a larger safety margin and strong operational controls.

7
00:00:50.000 --> 00:01:00.320
A six-digit code can be acceptable in some human-entered flows, but only with short lifetime, attempt limits, rate limits, monitoring, and account context.

8
00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:08.760
Encoding matters because tokens move through URLs, headers, cookies, databases, emails, logs, and support tools.

9
00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:14.940
Hex and base64url are common choices, but the team should understand the transport path.

10
00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:27.040
Avoid modulo bias, timestamp-based secrets, predictable seeds, counters, string slicing, and custom token generators that make a value look random without providing a meaningful guessing margin.

11
00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:30.120
Token generation is only the first step.

12
00:01:30.220 --> 00:01:37.580
The token still needs storage, comparison, expiration, revocation, and logging controls.

13
00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:47.840
A strong random value that is stored in plaintext forever, printed in logs, accepted after account disablement, or compared with careless behavior is still a weak token system.

14
00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:53.780
Secure generation provides the raw material; the lifecycle turns it into a reliable control.
